    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures). The specification is already fully satisfied by the current code. Here's what I verified:\n\n## Findings\n\n**All rejection responses (400, 409, 429) already return RFC7807 `application/problem+json`** with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\nThe mechanism is centralized in `ProblemDetails.build(...)` (`rest/escalation/ProblemDetails.java`), which produces a Spring `ProblemDetail` with:\n- `type` = `about:blank` (RFC7807 default)\n- `title` = exception simple name\n- `status` = the HTTP status (via `ProblemDetail.forStatus`)\n- `detail` = the message\n\nEvery rejection flows through an escalation handler that uses it, each wired via a YAML file in `officefloor/escalation/` and sending `ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>>` (which OfficeFloor serializes with the `application/problem+json` content type):\n\n| Status | Exceptions / handlers |\n|--------|----------------------|\n| **400** | `MethodArgumentNotValidException` (Validation), `InvalidAddress`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `InvalidRegistrationDate`, `InvalidTelephone` |\n| **409** | `DuplicateOwnerException`, `CityAtCapacityException` |\n| **429** | `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException` |\n\nI also confirmed the HTTP status codes are unchanged, and that no function class emits a 400/409/429 directly (they only produce success or 404 responses), so there's no rejection path bypassing the problem+json handlers.\n\n**Test result:** The acceptance test `coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (POST `/api/owners` with `city` removed \u2192 400) passes, asserting both `application/problem+json` content type and `$.status == 400`. The full suite builds and passes.\n\nNo code changes were required \u2014 the implementation already meets the spec and all tests are green.",
